          "session_id": "full-g-md-015",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My knees hurt most days and I have been told to lose weight. Is joint pain ever urgent, and what does guidance describe as core treatment?",
          "answer": "The urgency check for your description returned **routine**, but that is not a clearance. If your knee pain ever comes on suddenly, or you notice marked swelling, redness, warmth, fever, inability to bear weight, or a knee that locks or gives way, get medical advice promptly. Those are the features that would make knee pain urgent. I could not retrieve a knee-specific red-flag note, so please check this list with a clinician or a knee-specific guideline.\n\nFor treatment, the osteoarthritis reference (condition-osteoarthritis) describes structured exercise as the core treatment for affected joints. This combines muscle strengthening, improving range of movement, and aerobic activity. The guidance (guideline-osteoarthritis-management) says exercise helps regardless of x-ray severity, age, or how much pain you have at the start, and that some pain during tolerable exercise is not a sign of damage. It usually recommends starting with supervised exercise. The same reference says weight management is part of care where relevant, although my search did not return the passages that describe weight loss for knees, so I cannot tell you what they say in detail.\n\nMedicines are described as add-ons to exercise, not replacements. Topical anti-inflammatory creams are listed as a preferred first drug option for knees. Opioids are not recommended for this condition, and most bodies do not recommend glucosamine or chondroitin. I cannot give doses or name products, so please discuss options with a clinician.\n\nThe condition-osteoarthritis reference also says knee osteoarthritis is manageable over long periods and does not inevitably lead to joint replacement. This is general information, not a judgement about your knees. Only a clinician who examines you can say whether it applies to you.\n\nI did not find any disagreement between authorities on these points, but my search did not return the section on where authorities differ, so I cannot rule out differences. A useful next step is to ask a clinician whether your daily knee pain needs assessment, and whether a supervised exercise plan and weight advice would suit you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 16336.297321000075,
          "red_flag_matched": false,
          "red_flag_matched_raw": false,
